INT. HOUSEBOAT GALLEY - NIGHT

Cramped. Warm. A table set for four, lamp swaying on its chain. Rain ticks on the portholes.

ISADORA, 30s, serves a stew. Her brother CALLUM, 30s, lopes in sock feet, loose and smiling. Their mother PRUDENCE, 60s, sits ramrod straight at the head. Her husband-in-law WENDELL, 60s, quiet, folds a napkin into thirds.

They eat. Spoons clink. Prudence compliments the stew. Callum compliments Prudence. Wendell compliments the weather.

CALLUM
Good rain.

PRUDENCE
It keeps the river honest.

WENDELL
Pass the bread, Callum.

Callum passes it. Isadora refills a glass. Her hand is steady. She pours slow.

ISADORA
Mama, I've been meaning to ask.

PRUDENCE
Mm.

CALLUM
How's the boat's engine, Wendell?

WENDELL
Runs.

ISADORA
Mama, about the deed.

PRUDENCE
Not at supper.

CALLUM
Dora, leave it.

ISADORA
She sold it, didn't she.

WENDELL
Isadora, sit down.

ISADORA
I am sitting.

PRUDENCE
I sold the lot. Not the boat.

CALLUM
You what?

PRUDENCE
It was mine to sell.

ISADORA
(over her)
It was Daddy's.

WENDELL
(over Isadora)
It was never Leland's.

CALLUM
Wait. Wait. Who bought it?

PRUDENCE
Wendell did.

Silence. The lamp sways. He sets the napkin by his plate. He does not look at either of them. She picks up her glass and drinks the whole thing. He stands, goes to the porthole, and watches the rain.

ISADORA
Say something, Callum.

CALLUM
I'm having more stew.
